-- Hand-built, deliberately VALID instance of Sudoku.use (SOIL statements,
-- no Kodkod search) -- the fixed 6x6 board skeleton from Sudoku.properties'
-- [puzzle] section (1 Board, 6 Row, 6 Column, 6 Square, 36 Field objects,
-- wired up via BoardRows/BoardColumns/BoardSquares/BoardFields and
-- RowFields/ColumnFields/SquareFields exactly as in that file's Set{}
-- literals), with every Field given the value from an independently
-- computed, independently verified, fully-solved 6x6 grid.
--
-- The grid (rows top-to-bottom, columns left-to-right):
--   5 3 6 2 4 1
--   4 1 2 6 3 5
--   3 2 4 1 5 6
--   6 5 1 3 2 4
--   2 6 5 4 1 3
--   1 4 3 5 6 2
-- is the same solution already documented at the bottom of Sudoku.use as
-- the unique solution to that file's 10 given-clue invariants, originally
-- verified there by a standalone Python backtracking solver -- NOT Kodkod,
-- NOT this plugin, and NOT the witness KK-ModelValidator's own search
-- happens to find (see Sudoku.use's header and docs/kk-modelvalidator-port.md
-- for why that independence is the entire point of a validating fixture in
-- this suite). Before writing this fixture, that grid was independently
-- re-verified again here, separately, with a second small Python script
-- (kept for this task) that explicitly checks: every row is a permutation
-- of 1..6, every column is a permutation of 1..6, every 2x3 box is a
-- permutation of 1..6, and all 10 given clues (givenR1C3 .. givenR6C1 in
-- Sudoku.use) hold against the grid -- i.e. two independent verifications,
-- neither of them Kodkod, agree.
--
-- Respects both plugin workarounds documented in Sudoku.use's header:
--   - Field's fixed-but-large population (36) is only a problem for the
--     KK-ModelValidator MultiplicityTransformator bug when expressed as a
--     literal multiplicity string in the .use file itself ("Field[36]");
--     it is NOT a problem here, since plain SOIL !create statements don't
--     go through that transformator at all -- 36 separate "!create FieldN
--     : Field" statements below is the natural, unaffected way to build
--     this population by hand.
--   - Row.index/Column.index are set directly here via ordinary !set
--     statements (Row1.index := 1, etc.) -- this is completely unrelated
--     to, and unaffected by, the broken *Kodkod-properties* per-object
--     attribute-value binding ("RowN_index = V" lines in a .properties
--     file, see Sudoku.use's header) since SOIL's !set is USE's own plain
--     object-attribute assignment mechanism, not a Kodkod bound at all.
--
-- Every invariant in Sudoku.use (allowedValue, uniqueValuesRow,
-- uniqueValuesColumn, uniqueValuesSquare, allowedColumnIndex,
-- columnIndexUnique, allowedRowIndex, rowIndexUnique, and the 10
-- givenRxCy clues -- 18 invariants total) should report OK against this
-- state -- see valid-instance.cmd.

!create Board1 : Board

!create Row1 : Row
!set Row1.index := 1
!create Row2 : Row
!set Row2.index := 2
!create Row3 : Row
!set Row3.index := 3
!create Row4 : Row
!set Row4.index := 4
!create Row5 : Row
!set Row5.index := 5
!create Row6 : Row
!set Row6.index := 6

!create Column1 : Column
!set Column1.index := 1
